@*
    A tag picker with no interop: the popup is a real listbox in the DOM, the
    input owns the focus the whole time, and `aria-activedescendant` moves the
    reading cursor instead of the focus. That is what makes the keyboard route —
    Up/Down to walk the list, Enter to take one, Backspace on an empty input to
    drop the last chip, Escape to close — behave the same for a mouse user and a
    screen reader user.

    Two things the browser decides on its own, both in components.js and both
    by class. Which of those keys keep their default: it swallows
    Up/Down/Enter/Escape on `.tag-select__input` at the keydown, because a
    decision the server makes arrives one key too late — see OnKeyDownAsync.
    And where the open list lands: when `.tag-select__list` is rendered past
    the bottom of the nearest scroll container, that container is scrolled so
    the list shows, because a popup in a column's clipped overflow is one
    option and a scrollbar.

    An option may name a section it belongs to, and the open list draws those
    sections. Three things about how, because none of them is the obvious choice:

    The section is a real `role="group"` carrying a visible label, not a header row
    dressed up as one. Nothing here ever moves the focus, so a presentational
    heading would be a thing on screen that the keyboard route cannot reach and the
    reading cursor never lands on — a sighted reader would see the boundary and a
    screen reader user would cross it in silence. DataTable states the same
    rationale for its `th scope="colgroup"`: the element that labels the run has to
    be the element that says it does. `aria-labelledby` pointing at the label is
    what makes the group announce its name on the way in.

    Sections are runs rather than a `GroupBy`, and the walk itself is
    `SelectorOptionRuns` — shared with the native selects, which draw the same
    sections as `optgroup`s, and where the rule and the reason for it are stated.
    What belongs here is which list is walked: `Matches` rather than `Options`, so a
    section whose options all fell out of the query takes its heading with it,
    without anything having to check for that case.

    The index an option is identified by stays flat and stays keyed on `Matches`,
    so nesting some options one level deeper changes nothing about `_activeIndex`,
    the cycling arithmetic, or the id `aria-activedescendant` resolves. A section is
    a fact about the markup, not about the order the keyboard walks.

    An option with no group is left where it was, an unwrapped child of the listbox,
    so a list nobody grouped renders exactly the markup it rendered before groups
    existed.

    The query is bound with `@bind:get`/`@bind:set` on `oninput`, not a `value`
    attribute beside an `@oninput` handler. The difference is what the renderer
    believes the field holds. With `@bind` it records the text the browser reported
    before it renders again, so a render that lands after the next key compares
    against what is really in the field; with a bare `value` it compares against
    what it last drew, and a render slower than the typing wrote that older text
    back, so characters typed fast enough vanished. A long option list is what
    makes a render that slow, which is why `MaxOptions` exists beside it.

    `MaxOptions` draws the first so many matches and a line saying how many more
    there are. That line is a `role="none"` row the keyboard never lands on, and
    the input's `aria-describedby` points at it, so a screen reader hears that the
    list is cut short. The cursor cycles through what is drawn, because an index
    into rows that are not on screen would resolve to nothing.
*@

<div class="@ClassValue" data-testid="@TestId">
    @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Label))
    {
        <label class="field__label" for="@InputId">@Label</label>
    }

    <div class="tag-select__control">
        @foreach (var value in ShowSelected ? VisibleValues : Array.Empty<string>())
        {
            <TagChip Text="@LabelFor(value)"
                     Removable="true"
                     Disabled="@Disabled"
                     CssClass="tag-select__chip"
                     OnRemove="() => RemoveAsync(value)" />
        }
        @if (ShowSelected && HiddenCount > 0)
        {
            <span class="tag-select__overflow">+@HiddenCount</span>
        }

        <input @ref="_input"
               id="@InputId"
               class="tag-select__input"
               type="text"
               role="combobox"
               autocomplete="off"
               @bind:get="_query"
               @bind:set="SetQuery"
               @bind:event="oninput"
               placeholder="@Placeholder"
               disabled="@Disabled"
               aria-expanded="@(_open ? "true" : "false")"
               aria-controls="@ListId"
               aria-autocomplete="list"
               aria-activedescendant="@ActiveOptionId"
               aria-describedby="@(_open && HiddenMatchCount > 0 ? MoreId : null)"
               aria-label="@(string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Label) ? Placeholder : null)"
               @onfocus="() => _open = true"
               @onblur="OnBlur"
               @onkeydown="OnKeyDownAsync"
               @attributes="AdditionalAttributes" />
    </div>

    @if (_open && (Shown.Count > 0 || CanCreate))
    {
        var matches = Shown;
        var runs = SelectorOptionRuns.Of(matches);

        @* preventDefault on mousedown keeps the focus in the input, so the
           blur that closes the popup cannot fire before this click lands. *@
        RenderFragment<int> option = index =>
            @<li id="@OptionId(index)"
                 class="@OptionClass(index)"
                 role="option"
                 aria-selected="@(index == _activeIndex ? "true" : "false")"
                 @onmousedown:preventDefault="true"
                 @onclick="() => AddAsync(matches[index].Value)"><span class="tag-select__option-label" title="@matches[index].Label">@matches[index].Label</span>@if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(matches[index].Hint))
                 {
                     <span class="tag-select__hint" title="@matches[index].Hint">@matches[index].Hint</span>
                 }</li>;

        <ul id="@ListId" class="tag-select__list" role="listbox" aria-label="@(Label ?? "Tags")">
            @foreach (var run in runs)
            {
                if (run.Name is null)
                {
                    for (var i = run.Start; i < run.End; i++)
                    {
                        @option(i)
                    }
                }
                else
                {
                    var groupId = $"{InputId}-group-{run.Ordinal}";
                    <li class="tag-select__group" role="group" aria-labelledby="@groupId">
                        <span id="@groupId" class="tag-select__group-label">@run.Name</span>
                        <ul class="tag-select__group-list" role="none">
                            @for (var i = run.Start; i < run.End; i++)
                            {
                                @option(i)
                            }
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                }
            }
            @if (HiddenMatchCount > 0)
            {
                <li id="@MoreId" class="tag-select__more" role="none">@MoreLabel</li>
            }
            @if (CanCreate)
            {
                <li id="@OptionId(matches.Count)"
                    class="@OptionClass(matches.Count)"
                    role="option"
                    aria-selected="@(_activeIndex == matches.Count ? "true" : "false")"
                    @onmousedown:preventDefault="true"
                    @onclick="() => AddAsync(_query.Trim())">@CreateLabel</li>
            }
        </ul>
    }
</div>

@code {
    private readonly string _id = "tag-select-" + Guid.NewGuid().ToString("N");
    private string _query = string.Empty;
    private bool _open;
    private int _activeIndex = -1;
    private ElementReference _input;

    /// <summary>Puts the caret in the input. For a host that opens the picker on a
    /// press of its own: the field taking the focus is what opens the list, so the
    /// next key the reader types is the query.</summary>
    public ValueTask FocusAsync() => _input.FocusAsync();

    [Parameter]
    public IReadOnlyList<SelectorOption> Options { get; set; } = [];

    [Parameter]
    public IReadOnlyList<string> SelectedValues { get; set; } = [];

    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<IReadOnlyList<string>> SelectedValuesChanged { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? Label { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? Placeholder { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Lets the user commit whatever they typed as a new tag.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool AllowCreate { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public bool Disabled { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether the control draws the selected values as its own chips.
    /// False is for a host that already draws the selection itself, in a richer
    /// shape than a tag's — a Devbook reference with its title, status and a way to
    /// open it — so the picker is the adding half and nothing else. The selection
    /// is still what keeps an option from being offered twice, and Backspace no
    /// longer drops a value, because the value it would drop is not in the
    /// control for the reader to see go.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool ShowSelected { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>How many chips fit before the rest collapse into a "+n" count.
    /// Zero or less shows them all.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public int MaxVisible { get; set; }

    /// <summary>How many matching options the open list draws; the rest are counted
    /// on a line asking for a narrower query. Zero or less draws every match, which
    /// is the default and right for the short lists most hosts hand in. For a host
    /// whose list runs into the hundreds, where drawing every row on every
    /// keystroke is what makes typing lag.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public int MaxOptions { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether the query also matches an option's value, not only its
    /// label. For a host whose values are something a reader types, such as a
    /// path, rather than an id nobody sees. Off by default: a value that is an id
    /// would otherwise match on characters the reader never saw.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool MatchValue { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? Id { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? CssClass { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    [Parameter(CaptureUnmatchedValues = true)]
    public IReadOnlyDictionary<string, object>? AdditionalAttributes { get; set; }

    private string InputId => string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Id) ? _id : Id;

    private string ListId => InputId + "-list";

    private string OptionId(int index) => $"{InputId}-option-{index}";

    private string MoreId => InputId + "-more";

    private string ClassValue
    {
        get
        {
            var open = _open ? " tag-select--open" : string.Empty;
            var extra = string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(CssClass) ? string.Empty : " " + CssClass;
            return $"field tag-select{open}{extra}";
        }
    }

    private IReadOnlyList<string> VisibleValues =>
        MaxVisible > 0 && SelectedValues.Count > MaxVisible
            ? SelectedValues.Take(MaxVisible).ToList()
            : SelectedValues;

    private int HiddenCount => MaxVisible > 0 ? Math.Max(0, SelectedValues.Count - MaxVisible) : 0;

    /// <summary>What the matches were last worked out from, so a render that reads
    /// them several times filters the options once. Compared by reference: a host
    /// that hands in a new list or a new selection is asking a new question.</summary>
    private (IReadOnlyList<SelectorOption> Options, IReadOnlyList<string> Selected, string Query, bool MatchValue)? _matchedFor;

    private List<SelectorOption> _matches = [];

    private List<SelectorOption> Matches
    {
        get
        {
            if (_matchedFor is { } asked
                && ReferenceEquals(asked.Options, Options)
                && ReferenceEquals(asked.Selected, SelectedValues)
                && string.Equals(asked.Query, _query, StringComparison.Ordinal)
                && asked.MatchValue == MatchValue)
            {
                return _matches;
            }

            var query = _query.Trim();
            var selected = new HashSet<string>(SelectedValues, StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase);

            _matches = Options
                .Where(option => !selected.Contains(option.Value))
                .Where(option => query.Length == 0
                    || option.Label.Contains(query, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
                    || (MatchValue && option.Value.Contains(query, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)))
                .ToList();
            _matchedFor = (Options, SelectedValues, _query, MatchValue);
            return _matches;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>The matches the open list draws: all of them, or the first
    /// <see cref="MaxOptions"/>. Every index the keyboard walks is into this.</summary>
    private List<SelectorOption> Shown =>
        MaxOptions > 0 && Matches.Count > MaxOptions ? Matches.GetRange(0, MaxOptions) : Matches;

    private int HiddenMatchCount => MaxOptions > 0 ? Math.Max(0, Matches.Count - MaxOptions) : 0;

    private string MoreLabel => $"{HiddenMatchCount} more — keep typing to narrow the list";

    private bool CanCreate =>
        AllowCreate
        && !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_query)
        && !SelectedValues.Contains(_query.Trim(), StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
        && !Options.Any(option => string.Equals(option.Value, _query.Trim(), StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase));

    private int OptionCount => Shown.Count + (CanCreate ? 1 : 0);

    private string CreateLabel => $"Add \"{_query.Trim()}\"";

    private string? ActiveOptionId => _open && _activeIndex >= 0 && _activeIndex < OptionCount
        ? OptionId(_activeIndex)
        : null;

    private string OptionClass(int index) => index == _activeIndex
        ? "tag-select__option tag-select__option--active"
        : "tag-select__option";

    private string LabelFor(string value) =>
        Options.FirstOrDefault(option => string.Equals(option.Value, value, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))?.Label
        ?? value;

    private void SetQuery(string? value)
    {
        _query = value ?? string.Empty;
        _open = true;
        _activeIndex = OptionCount > 0 ? 0 : -1;
    }

    private async Task OnKeyDownAsync(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        // Arrow keys would scroll the popup's ancestor and Enter would submit a
        // surrounding form, so those are the keys the browser swallows — and only
        // those. Which keys is decided in components.js, on the `.tag-select__input`
        // class, and deliberately not here: nothing this handler sets can reach the
        // browser before the default it wants to stop has already run. It used to
        // set a flag bound to `@onkeydown:preventDefault`, and the flag was rendered
        // after this handler returned, so it applied to the *next* key — the arrow
        // was never prevented and the letter typed after it was eaten.
        switch (e.Key)
        {
            case "ArrowDown":
                _open = true;
                if (OptionCount > 0) _activeIndex = (_activeIndex + 1) % OptionCount;
                break;

            case "ArrowUp":
                _open = true;
                if (OptionCount > 0) _activeIndex = (_activeIndex <= 0 ? OptionCount : _activeIndex) - 1;
                break;

            case "Enter":
                if (!_open || _activeIndex < 0 || _activeIndex >= OptionCount) break;
                var shown = Shown;
                await AddAsync(_activeIndex < shown.Count ? shown[_activeIndex].Value : _query.Trim());
                break;

            case "Escape":
                _open = false;
                _activeIndex = -1;
                break;

            case "Backspace":
                if (!ShowSelected || _query.Length > 0 || SelectedValues.Count == 0) break;
                await RemoveAsync(SelectedValues[^1]);
                break;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Closes the popup when focus genuinely leaves the control.
    ///
    /// There was no way to dismiss it with a mouse at all: the list opened on
    /// focus, stayed open after a pick, and only Escape closed it — so it sat
    /// over the page covering whatever was underneath. Options preventDefault
    /// their mousedown, so this only fires for a click outside or a Tab away.
    /// </summary>
    private void OnBlur()
    {
        _open = false;
        _activeIndex = -1;
    }

    private async Task AddAsync(string value)
    {
        if (Disabled || string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(value)) return;
        if (SelectedValues.Contains(value, StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)) return;

        var next = SelectedValues.ToList();
        next.Add(value);

        _query = string.Empty;
        _activeIndex = -1;
        // Taking an option answers the question the popup was asking. Typing or
        // ArrowDown reopens it, so picking several in a row still costs one key.
        _open = false;

        await CommitAsync(next);
    }

    private async Task RemoveAsync(string value)
    {
        if (Disabled) return;

        var next = SelectedValues
            .Where(existing => !string.Equals(existing, value, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
            .ToList();

        if (next.Count == SelectedValues.Count) return;

        await CommitAsync(next);
    }

    private Task CommitAsync(List<string> next)
    {
        SelectedValues = next;
        return SelectedValuesChanged.InvokeAsync(next);
    }
}
